# Build artifacts from topo-build
**/build/
**/.topo-cache/

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why explicit enumeration?
#
# Each benchmark dir <name>/ produces a vanilla binary at
# <name>/<name> (same name as parent dir, no extension). On macOS
# Mach-O executables carry no extension, so a generic gitignore
# pattern can't distinguish "build-output binary" from a source file
# or subdirectory by extension matching alone.
#
# We can't write `same-name-as-parent-dir` in glob syntax, so the
# strategy is: enumerate by directory-name family. The variant
# binaries (*_base, *_forced, *_baseline, *_baseline_*) ARE
# enumerable by suffix and live above. The bare-name binary needs
# either a per-dir entry or a family wildcard like `*/<prefix>*`.
#
# When adding a new benchmark dir, append its bare-name pattern
# below — or, better, use a `*/<prefix>*` wildcard if it joins an
# existing family.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------

# Variant binaries (enumerable by suffix)
*_baseline
*_baseline_*
*_base
*_forced

# Bare-name binaries (per-dir / per-family)
*/adaptive_parallel
*/auxiliary_types
# class_template family: class_template, class_template_comptime, _constraint, _crtp, _templates, _types, _variadic
*/class_template*
*/data_layout_perf
*/data_layout_force_soa
*/hello_visibility
*/hints
*/indirection
*/ir_embedding
*/lifetime_arena
*/lifetime_forced
*/loop_parallel
*/multi_return
*/multireturn
*/observability
*/ownership
*/parallel_runtime
*/pipeline
*/stages
*/cross_module_adapter
*/operator_pipeline
*/variadic_template
*/visibility_adapter
*/mixed_lang
*/priority
*/obfuscation
*/loop_reduction
# friendly/unfriendly families (lifetime_*, ownership_*, parallel_*, and any
# future bench) — durable cross-family wildcards. The `*/` prefix matches the
# <dir>/<name>_friendly build binary two levels deep, NOT the one-level source
# dir, so tracked sources under e.g. lifetime_friendly/ stay tracked.
*/*_friendly
*/*_unfriendly

# Rust benchmark build output (e.g. mixed_lang/rust_lib/). The crate is a
# zero-dependency staticlib, so its Cargo.lock pins nothing for
# reproducibility (lockfiles are worth committing only for crates that have
# external dependencies) — safe to ignore as build noise.
*/rust_lib/target/
*/rust_lib/Cargo.lock

# Diagnostic artifacts that `topo build` / `topo-prof` may emit alongside
# bench binaries. Same script produces an `.exe` on Windows and an ELF on
# Linux, so a wildcard catches them without per-bench enumeration.
*/v_vanilla
*/*.ll
*/*.dSYM
*/*.topo-passes
